    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d367">

                <pb facs="0649" n="635" sameAs="frus:frus1949v05/pg635"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 Kirk, Alan G.: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                            Embassy in the Soviet Union
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-08-01T14:00:00-04:00">August 1, 1949—2
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>544. Instr going forward next day or two in reply your 1813 July 21. In this
                    connection tele whether top secret Embtel
                    1053 Apr 5, 1948 <pb facs="0650" n="636" xml:id="pg636"/> still retained in
                        Emb files.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d367-f1"/>
                    This is report of Stalin-Smith conversation and if not available will be sent
                    you immed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d367-f1" n="1" xml:id="d367-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram 1053 reported the interview on April 4, 1946, that took place between
                    Stalin and Ambassador Walter Bedell Smith, <ref target="frus1946v06#pg_732">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1946, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">vi</hi>, pp. 732</ref>–<ref
                        target="frus1946v06#pg_736">736</ref>. The Embassy answered in telegram 1912
                    from Moscow on August 2, 1949, that it did possess a copy of the telegram.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
